{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792448709,"nonce":"6GlwGlz6syW2bK2WX4joToDoIqo=","message":"Reduce transient allocations while switching tabs\n\nThe user reports 58 open tabs at 691 MB after afd2e51's document/view separation. Follow-up profiling found the remaining small-file switch cost was dominated by constructing viewport-sized CodeEditor/CodeView row pools, including empty rows and their gutter controls/reactive bindings. Ashui cb18dbc caps slots at the visible document length while preserving the viewport/overscan bound for long sources. It also clamps offsets after folding or replacing a source, so a previously scrolled editor cannot remain beyond the new content.\n\nRayDE adopts cb18dbcc093b77ffa8c55bd3cb911d6e7b240228, including the independently landed native tree-map FxHash change ac2d7d6. The shared native build was reused; no second Cargo target was built for this work. The existing CSS compiler was reused. A Haxe-only backport branch rayde-row-pool exists at 1c4e9ac in Ashui; its unused source checkout was removed when the shared main build became available.\n\nFinal paired comparison: both programs use the same new native library (d4d2787179b9d3b4b79e5328df6fc8c62b59ff2e7cf753f5b0dc17ef186c4d89), same ten short code files, Git sidebar/history, and sequential runs. The baseline uses the previous CodeEditor; the candidate uses the bounded pools. Across 33 switches, median allocated bytes fall 39.34 -\u003e 15.04 MiB and median three-frame work 141.2 -\u003e 61.3 ms. This is an offscreen fixture workload, not a latency guarantee for arbitrary files. Baseline collected footprint 166.8 -\u003e 142.7 MiB, open-tabs-collected 478.8 -\u003e 423.1 MiB, final post-close/drained 307.2 -\u003e 297.5 MiB. Allocation counters include native-handle pressure; physical footprint is from vmmap.\n\nCodeView's empty pool falls from 25 rows to 1. With a six-line file active, CodeEditor has six slots, alongside one empty Output slot. At history-active collection: watches 741 -\u003e 597. After closing: watches 462 -\u003e 318. Reclose cycles 0/1/2 stay at the same counts and 324 layout nodes, zero cached documents/editors/canvases, zero disposed component hosts. No accumulating closed editor view was observed. Remaining process footprint is still open; AIR statistics report about 16 MiB of live canonical bodies / 8 MiB resident strong cache, which does not account for all of the remainder.\n\nThe sampler now uses temporary file acknowledgements and sleeps between polls; RAYDE_PROBE_PROGRAM selects a comparison bytecode without overwriting the current build. Both comparison runs used that protocol. A two-second GC stop-world deferral still occurs during shutdown after all 70 checkpoints, so the earlier stdin-only attribution was insufficient; this remains a separate investigation. No change was made to the runtime collector.\n\nArtifacts: bin/interaction-row-cap-final-before and bin/interaction-row-cap-final-after contain all 70 records, native footprint summaries and logs. Ashui CodeComponents passes 274 checks and CodeStateLifecycle 20 checks. RayDE Basic/Full/Documents/Git pass in both schemes, with previews under bin/row-cap-snapshots. The Git test now waits for refresh to finish before clicking Fetch after changing settings, rather than clicking a temporarily disabled action. Native bin/rayde and bin/RayDE.app are rebuilt for preview.","files":null}]}